// Built from tools/qsrc/s1_batch1.py. Append-only.
Q.push(
["Human development", "A healthy 80-year-old man has pulmonary function tests as part of a research study. Compared with a healthy 25-year-old, which finding is an expected normal change of aging?", ["Increased residual volume with decreased vital capacity", "Increased lean body mass", "Increased glomerular filtration rate", "Increased time spent in REM sleep", "Increased maximal heart rate during exercise"], 0, "With age, lungs lose elastic recoil and the chest wall stiffens, so air is trapped: residual volume rises and vital capacity falls (TLC stays about the same). GFR, maximal heart rate, lean body mass and REM sleep all decrease with age.", 1, "F", "Physiology"],
["Human development", "An infant sits without support, crawls, says \"mama\" and \"dada\" without meaning, and cries when held by strangers. A pincer grasp is just emerging. What is the most likely age?", ["4 months", "15 months", "24 months", "2 months", "9 months"], 4, "Stranger anxiety, crawling, nonspecific \"mama/dada\" and an emerging pincer grasp fit 9 months. At 15 months a child walks and uses a few words with meaning; at 24 months a child runs and uses 2-word phrases.", 1, "F", "Behavioral science"],
["Blood & immune", "A 32-year-old woman with heavy menstrual periods has fatigue. Hemoglobin is 9.1 g/dL, MCV 71 fL, ferritin low, and total iron-binding capacity high. What is the most likely diagnosis?", ["Sideroblastic anemia", "Iron deficiency anemia", "Anemia of chronic disease", "Beta-thalassemia minor", "Lead poisoning"], 1, "Microcytic anemia with low ferritin and high TIBC is iron deficiency, here from menstrual blood loss. Anemia of chronic disease has high ferritin and low TIBC. Thalassemia minor has normal iron studies.", 1, "F", "Pathology"],
["Blood & immune", "A 10-year-old boy has jaundice, splenomegaly and anemia. His father had a splenectomy. The smear shows small round red cells without central pallor, MCHC is increased, and the direct Coombs test is negative. A defect in which protein is most likely?", ["CD55 and CD59 (GPI-anchored proteins)", "Beta-globin chain", "Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase", "Pyruvate kinase", "Spectrin or ankyrin"], 4, "Hereditary spherocytosis is usually autosomal dominant and caused by defects in red cell membrane skeleton proteins (ankyrin, spectrin, band 3). Cells lose membrane, become spherocytes and are trapped in the spleen. CD55/CD59 loss causes PNH.", 1, "F", "Pathology"],
["Blood & immune", "A 64-year-old man on unfractionated heparin after knee surgery has a platelet count fall from 260,000 to 105,000/mm3 on day 6 and develops a new deep vein thrombosis. What is the mechanism?", ["Direct heparin toxicity to megakaryocytes", "Heparin inhibition of thrombopoietin", "Consumption of platelets by disseminated intravascular coagulation", "IgG antibodies against complexes of heparin and platelet factor 4", "Splenic sequestration of platelets"], 3, "Heparin-induced thrombocytopenia type 2 appears 5 to 10 days after starting heparin. Antibodies to heparin-PF4 activate platelets, causing thrombocytopenia AND thrombosis. Stop all heparin and start a non-heparin anticoagulant such as argatroban.", 1, "C", "Immunology"],
["Blood & immune", "A patient starts warfarin for atrial fibrillation. The prothrombin time rises within the first day or two, before the full anticoagulant effect develops. A fall in which clotting factor explains this early rise?", ["Factor IX", "Factor X", "Factor II (prothrombin)", "Factor VIII", "Factor VII"], 4, "Warfarin blocks vitamin K epoxide reductase, reducing factors II, VII, IX and X. Factor VII has the shortest half-life (about 6 hours), so it falls first and raises the PT early. Full anticoagulation needs prothrombin to fall, which takes days. Factor VIII is not vitamin K dependent.", 1, "C", "Pharmacology"],
["Blood & immune", "A 10-month-old boy has had recurrent otitis media and two pneumonias with encapsulated bacteria since age 6 months. Serum IgG, IgA and IgM are all very low, and flow cytometry shows almost no CD19+ cells. A defect in which gene is most likely?", ["Adenosine deaminase", "Bruton tyrosine kinase (BTK)", "CD40 ligand", "WAS (Wiskott-Aldrich) gene", "NADPH oxidase"], 1, "X-linked agammaglobulinemia: BTK defects block B cell maturation, so B cells and all antibody classes are absent. Infections start after about 6 months, when maternal IgG wanes. CD40L defects cause hyper-IgM syndrome; NADPH oxidase defects cause chronic granulomatous disease.", 1, "F", "Immunology"],
["Blood & immune", "Minutes into her first blood transfusion, a woman with frequent sinus infections and giardiasis develops hypotension, wheezing and urticaria. What underlying condition most likely explains this reaction?", ["Common variable immunodeficiency", "Bruton agammaglobulinemia", "ABO incompatibility", "Selective IgA deficiency", "Hereditary angioedema"], 3, "IgA-deficient patients can form anti-IgA antibodies and have anaphylaxis to IgA in donor blood. Future transfusions should use washed or IgA-deficient products. It is the most common primary immunodeficiency and often causes sinopulmonary and GI (Giardia) infections.", 1, "F", "Immunology"],
["Blood & immune", "A 55-year-old man has a white cell count of 140,000/mm3 with neutrophils at all stages of maturation, basophilia and splenomegaly. Cytogenetics show t(9;22). Which drug directly targets the cause of his disease?", ["Imatinib", "Hydroxyurea", "Trastuzumab", "Rituximab", "All-trans retinoic acid"], 0, "Chronic myeloid leukemia is driven by the BCR-ABL fusion gene from the Philadelphia chromosome, which makes a constantly active tyrosine kinase. Imatinib inhibits it. All-trans retinoic acid treats APL with t(15;17).", 1, "F", "Pharmacology"],
["Blood & immune", "A 22-year-old man has painless cervical lymphadenopathy, night sweats and fever. Biopsy shows large binucleate cells with prominent \"owl-eye\" nucleoli in a background of reactive inflammatory cells. Which markers are these cells most likely to express?", ["CD10 and BCL-6", "CD15 and CD30", "CD19 and CD20 only", "CD34 and TdT", "CD3 and CD4"], 1, "Reed-Sternberg cells of classic Hodgkin lymphoma are CD15+ and CD30+. Nodular sclerosis is the most common subtype in young adults. TdT and CD34 mark lymphoblasts (ALL).", 1, "F", "Pathology"],
["Blood & immune", "Shortly after delivery, a woman develops bleeding from IV sites and the uterus. PT and PTT are prolonged, platelets are low, fibrinogen is low and D-dimer is high. The smear shows schistocytes. What is the most likely diagnosis?", ["Disseminated intravascular coagulation", "Von Willebrand disease", "Hemolytic uremic syndrome", "Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura", "Immune thrombocytopenic purpura"], 0, "DIC consumes clotting factors and platelets: PT and PTT rise, fibrinogen falls, and fibrin breakdown (D-dimer) rises. Obstetric complications such as amniotic fluid embolism are classic triggers. TTP and HUS have normal PT and PTT.", 1, "F", "Pathology"],
["Blood & immune", "A 35-year-old woman has fever, confusion, petechiae and rising creatinine. Platelets are 18,000/mm3, the smear shows schistocytes, and PT and PTT are normal. What is the underlying mechanism?", ["Consumption of clotting factors", "Deficient activity of the vWF-cleaving protease ADAMTS13", "Absent glycoprotein Ib on platelets", "Shiga toxin damage to renal endothelium", "Antibodies against platelet GPIIb/IIIa"], 1, "TTP is usually caused by autoantibodies that inhibit ADAMTS13. Uncleaved large vWF multimers cause platelet microthrombi, hemolysis with schistocytes, and organ damage, with normal PT/PTT. Treat urgently with plasma exchange. Antibodies to GPIIb/IIIa cause ITP.", 1, "C", "Pathology"],
["Blood & immune", "A 45-year-old vegan man has fatigue and numbness of his feet with reduced vibration and position sense. Hemoglobin is low with MCV 115 fL and hypersegmented neutrophils. Methylmalonic acid and homocysteine are both elevated. Which vitamin is deficient?", ["Folate (vitamin B9)", "Thiamine (vitamin B1)", "Cobalamin (vitamin B12)", "Niacin (vitamin B3)", "Pyridoxine (vitamin B6)"], 2, "B12 is a cofactor for methionine synthase (homocysteine to methionine) and methylmalonyl-CoA mutase. Raised methylmalonic acid and neurologic signs (dorsal column damage) separate B12 from folate deficiency, which raises homocysteine only.", 1, "F", "Biochemistry"],
["Nervous system & behavior", "A 68-year-old man has a resting tremor, slow movements, cogwheel rigidity and a shuffling gait. Loss of which neurons is most responsible for his signs?", ["Cholinergic neurons of the nucleus basalis of Meynert", "GABAergic neurons of the caudate nucleus", "Purkinje cells of the cerebellum", "Motor neurons of the anterior horn", "Dopaminergic neurons of the substantia nigra pars compacta"], 4, "Parkinson disease destroys dopaminergic neurons of the substantia nigra pars compacta; surviving neurons contain Lewy bodies (alpha-synuclein). Caudate loss is Huntington disease; nucleus basalis loss is seen in Alzheimer disease.", 1, "F", "Pathology"],
["Nervous system & behavior", "A 30-year-old woman has drooping eyelids and double vision that worsen toward evening and improve with rest. A chest CT shows an anterior mediastinal mass. What is the cause of her weakness?", ["Autoantibodies against presynaptic voltage-gated calcium channels", "Inhibition of acetylcholine release by a bacterial toxin", "Loss of upper motor neurons", "Demyelination of peripheral nerves", "Autoantibodies against postsynaptic nicotinic acetylcholine receptors"], 4, "Myasthenia gravis is a type II hypersensitivity against the nicotinic ACh receptor; it is linked to thymoma and thymic hyperplasia. Antibodies to presynaptic calcium channels cause Lambert-Eaton syndrome, where strength improves with repeated use.", 1, "F", "Immunology"],
["Nervous system & behavior", "A man with long-term heavy alcohol use is brought in confused, with nystagmus, eye movement palsies and an ataxic gait. The vitamin he lacks is a cofactor for which enzyme?", ["Pyruvate dehydrogenase", "Methylmalonyl-CoA mutase", "Pyruvate carboxylase", "Glutamate decarboxylase", "Lysyl oxidase"], 0, "Wernicke encephalopathy is thiamine (B1) deficiency. Thiamine pyrophosphate is a cofactor for pyruvate dehydrogenase, alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase, transketolase and branched-chain ketoacid dehydrogenase. Give thiamine before glucose. Biotin serves pyruvate carboxylase; B12 serves methylmalonyl-CoA mutase.", 1, "F", "Biochemistry"],
["Nervous system & behavior", "A right-handed 70-year-old woman suddenly develops weakness of the right face and arm, with the leg less affected. She understands speech but speaks in short, effortful phrases. Which artery is most likely occluded?", ["Left anterior cerebral artery", "Basilar artery", "Right middle cerebral artery", "Left middle cerebral artery", "Left posterior cerebral artery"], 3, "Face and arm weakness greater than leg points to the lateral motor cortex, supplied by the MCA. Expressive (Broca) aphasia means the dominant left hemisphere. The ACA supplies the medial cortex, so its stroke affects the leg most.", 1, "F", "Anatomy"],
["Nervous system & behavior", "After a stab wound that cuts the left half of the spinal cord at T10, which pattern of deficits is expected below the lesion?", ["Left leg weakness and loss of vibration sense; right-sided loss of pain and temperature", "Loss of pain and temperature on both sides only", "Right leg weakness and loss of vibration sense; left-sided loss of pain and temperature", "Bilateral leg weakness with loss of all sensation", "Left-sided loss of all sensation with normal strength"], 0, "Brown-Sequard syndrome: corticospinal tract and dorsal columns cross in the brainstem, so their loss is on the same side. The spinothalamic tract crosses near its entry level, so pain and temperature are lost on the opposite side.", 1, "F", "Anatomy"],
["Nervous system & behavior", "A woman taking fluoxetine is given linezolid for a resistant infection. Hours later she is agitated and sweating, with fever, diarrhea, hyperreflexia and inducible clonus. Which drug is the specific treatment?", ["Naloxone", "Bromocriptine", "Cyproheptadine", "Dantrolene", "Physostigmine"], 2, "This is serotonin syndrome; linezolid is a weak MAO inhibitor. Cyproheptadine is a 5-HT2A antagonist. Dantrolene treats malignant hyperthermia; bromocriptine treats neuroleptic malignant syndrome, which has rigidity rather than clonus.", 1, "F", "Pharmacology"],
["Nervous system & behavior", "A woman with bipolar disorder has stable mood on her medication but now passes large volumes of dilute urine. Her urine stays dilute after desmopressin. Which drug most likely caused this?", ["Carbamazepine", "Valproate", "Lithium", "Quetiapine", "Lamotrigine"], 2, "Lithium causes nephrogenic diabetes insipidus by reducing collecting duct response to ADH, so desmopressin does not help. Other lithium effects: hypothyroidism, tremor, and Ebstein anomaly if taken in pregnancy. Carbamazepine can cause SIADH, the opposite problem.", 1, "F", "Pharmacology"],
["Nervous system & behavior", "A 22-year-old man has heard voices commenting on his actions and believed his neighbors are spying on him for 3 months. He has no mood episodes and no substance use. What is the most likely diagnosis?", ["Delusional disorder", "Schizoaffective disorder", "Schizophrenia", "Schizophreniform disorder", "Brief psychotic disorder"], 3, "The diagnosis depends on duration: brief psychotic disorder lasts under 1 month, schizophreniform disorder 1 to 6 months, and schizophrenia more than 6 months. Delusional disorder has delusions without hallucinations or other psychotic features.", 1, "F", "Behavioral science"],
["Nervous system & behavior", "A 50-year-old woman has a sudden severe headache. Her right eye is turned down and out, the right lid droops, and the right pupil is dilated and does not react to light. Compression of which structure is most likely?", ["Right sympathetic chain by a lung tumor", "Right oculomotor nerve by a posterior communicating artery aneurysm", "Right optic nerve by a pituitary tumor", "Right trochlear nerve in the cavernous sinus", "Right abducens nerve by raised intracranial pressure"], 1, "Parasympathetic pupil fibers run on the outside of CN III, so compression (classically a PComm aneurysm) dilates the pupil early. Diabetic CN III palsy affects the center of the nerve and usually spares the pupil. Sympathetic loss causes a small pupil (Horner).", 1, "F", "Anatomy"],
["Nervous system & behavior", "A 35-year-old man has fever, headache and neck stiffness. CSF shows 2,000 white cells/mm3, mostly neutrophils, low glucose and high protein. Gram stain shows lancet-shaped gram-positive diplococci. What is the most likely organism?", ["Haemophilus influenzae type b", "Streptococcus agalactiae", "Neisseria meningitidis", "Streptococcus pneumoniae", "Listeria monocytogenes"], 3, "Lancet-shaped gram-positive diplococci are S. pneumoniae, the most common cause of bacterial meningitis in adults. N. meningitidis is a gram-negative diplococcus; Listeria is a gram-positive rod, important in neonates, the elderly and the immunocompromised.", 1, "F", "Microbiology"],
["Nervous system & behavior", "A 40-year-old man has chorea, irritability and memory decline. His father developed the same disease at age 50. Imaging shows atrophy of the caudate nuclei. Which genetic finding is expected?", ["Expanded CAG trinucleotide repeats in the HTT gene", "Expanded GAA repeats in the frataxin gene", "Expanded CGG repeats in the FMR1 gene", "Deletion in the SMN1 gene", "A point mutation in the APP gene"], 0, "Huntington disease is autosomal dominant with CAG repeat expansion. Repeats can grow when passed down, especially from the father, so symptoms start earlier in later generations (anticipation). CGG is fragile X; GAA is Friedreich ataxia.", 1, "F", "Genetics"],
["Nervous system & behavior", "A 76-year-old woman has gradually worsening memory over 2 years and now gets lost in her own neighborhood. Her physician starts donepezil. What is the mechanism of this drug?", ["Removes amyloid plaques from the brain", "Increases dopamine release", "Blocks NMDA glutamate receptors", "Inhibits acetylcholinesterase in the brain", "Inhibits monoamine oxidase B"], 3, "Alzheimer disease reduces cholinergic neurons (nucleus basalis of Meynert). Donepezil, rivastigmine and galantamine inhibit acetylcholinesterase to raise ACh. Memantine is the NMDA antagonist used in moderate to severe disease.", 1, "F", "Pharmacology"],
["Musculoskeletal & skin", "A 5-year-old boy has trouble climbing stairs and uses his hands to push up his thighs when rising from the floor. His calves look enlarged and serum creatine kinase is very high. What is the most likely cause?", ["A frameshift deletion in the dystrophin gene", "A nonframeshift deletion in the dystrophin gene", "CTG repeat expansion in the DMPK gene", "Antibodies against muscle acetylcholine receptors", "A deletion in the SMN1 gene"], 0, "Duchenne muscular dystrophy is X-linked; frameshift deletions make no functional dystrophin, so muscle fibers break down and are replaced by fat (pseudohypertrophy). Nonframeshift deletions cause the milder Becker form. CTG repeats cause myotonic dystrophy.", 1, "F", "Genetics"],
["Musculoskeletal & skin", "A child has had several fractures after minor falls. Her sclerae are blue and her father has hearing loss and a history of many fractures. Defective synthesis of which molecule is most likely?", ["Type IV collagen", "Type III collagen", "Type I collagen", "Fibrillin-1", "Type II collagen"], 2, "Osteogenesis imperfecta is usually an autosomal dominant defect in COL1A1 or COL1A2. Type I collagen is in bone, sclera, teeth, tendon and the middle ear bones. Type IV defects cause Alport syndrome; fibrillin defects cause Marfan syndrome.", 1, "F", "Biochemistry"],
["Musculoskeletal & skin", "A 55-year-old man has had three attacks of a hot, swollen first toe joint. Aspirate showed needle-shaped crystals that are negatively birefringent. For long-term prevention he starts allopurinol. What is its mechanism?", ["Inhibits cyclooxygenase", "Increases renal uric acid excretion", "Inhibits microtubule polymerization in neutrophils", "Converts uric acid to allantoin", "Inhibits xanthine oxidase"], 4, "Allopurinol and febuxostat inhibit xanthine oxidase, lowering uric acid production. Colchicine blocks microtubules (acute attacks), probenecid increases excretion, and pegloticase converts uric acid to allantoin.", 1, "F", "Pharmacology"],
["Musculoskeletal & skin", "After a difficult delivery with shoulder dystocia, a newborn's right arm hangs adducted and internally rotated, with the elbow extended and wrist flexed. Which part of the brachial plexus is injured?", ["Radial nerve at the spiral groove", "Upper trunk (C5 and C6)", "Long thoracic nerve", "Lower trunk (C8 and T1)", "Posterior cord"], 1, "Erb palsy (\"waiter's tip\") comes from stretching the upper trunk when the head is pulled away from the shoulder. Abductors, lateral rotators and biceps lose function. Lower trunk injury (Klumpke) causes a claw hand.", 1, "F", "Anatomy"],
["Musculoskeletal & skin", "A 45-year-old woman has morning stiffness lasting over an hour and symmetric swelling of the metacarpophalangeal and proximal interphalangeal joints. Which antibody is most specific for her likely disease?", ["Rheumatoid factor", "Antinuclear antibody", "Anti-centromere antibody", "Anti-double-stranded DNA antibody", "Anti-cyclic citrullinated peptide antibody"], 4, "Rheumatoid arthritis is linked to HLA-DR4. Anti-CCP is the most specific test; rheumatoid factor (IgM against IgG Fc) is more often positive but less specific. Anti-dsDNA is specific for lupus; anti-centromere suggests limited systemic sclerosis.", 1, "F", "Immunology"],
["Musculoskeletal & skin", "A 60-year-old man treated with hydralazine for hypertension develops joint pain, a pleural effusion and a positive ANA. His kidneys are normal. Which antibody is most likely positive?", ["Anti-Scl-70 antibody", "Anti-double-stranded DNA antibody", "Anti-Jo-1 antibody", "Anti-Smith antibody", "Anti-histone antibody"], 4, "Drug-induced lupus (hydralazine, procainamide, isoniazid) gives anti-histone antibodies and rarely involves the kidneys or brain. It improves after the drug is stopped. Anti-Smith and anti-dsDNA point to systemic lupus.", 1, "F", "Immunology"],
["Musculoskeletal & skin", "A 30-year-old man has well-demarcated salmon-colored plaques with silvery scale on his elbows and knees, and pitting of the nails. Which histologic finding is expected in a skin biopsy?", ["Noncaseating granulomas in the dermis", "Intercellular edema of the epidermis (spongiosis)", "Parakeratosis with neutrophil collections in the stratum corneum", "Subepidermal blisters with IgG along the basement membrane", "Acantholysis with intraepidermal blisters"], 2, "Psoriasis shows acanthosis, parakeratosis (retained nuclei in the stratum corneum) and Munro microabscesses. Acantholysis is pemphigus vulgaris; linear basement membrane IgG with subepidermal blisters is bullous pemphigoid; spongiosis is eczema.", 1, "F", "Pathology"],
["Musculoskeletal & skin", "A 50-year-old woman has a changing pigmented lesion removed and it is diagnosed as melanoma. Which feature is the most important predictor of metastasis?", ["Depth of invasion (Breslow thickness)", "Number of nevi on the body", "Diameter of the lesion", "Degree of pigmentation", "Patient's skin type"], 0, "Melanoma's risk of spread depends mostly on how deep it grows into the dermis (vertical growth phase). The ABCDE features help find melanoma but do not predict prognosis as well as depth.", 1, "F", "Pathology"],
["Musculoskeletal & skin", "A pregnant woman has numbness of her thumb, index and middle fingers that wakes her at night, and weakness of thumb opposition. Tapping the front of her wrist reproduces the tingling. Which nerve is compressed?", ["Radial nerve", "Musculocutaneous nerve", "Ulnar nerve", "Anterior interosseous nerve", "Median nerve"], 4, "Carpal tunnel syndrome compresses the median nerve under the flexor retinaculum. It is linked to pregnancy, hypothyroidism, diabetes and repetitive use. Sensation over the thenar eminence is spared because the palmar cutaneous branch runs above the tunnel.", 1, "F", "Anatomy"],
["Musculoskeletal & skin", "A 68-year-old woman with osteoporosis is started on alendronate and told to take it with a full glass of water and stay upright for 30 minutes. What is the drug's mechanism?", ["It blocks RANKL as a monoclonal antibody", "It binds bone mineral and inhibits osteoclast activity", "It stimulates osteoblasts as a parathyroid hormone analog", "It activates estrogen receptors in bone", "It increases intestinal calcium absorption"], 1, "Bisphosphonates are pyrophosphate analogs that bind hydroxyapatite and cause osteoclast apoptosis. Staying upright prevents pill esophagitis. Teriparatide is the PTH analog; denosumab blocks RANKL; raloxifene is a SERM.", 1, "F", "Pharmacology"],
["Cardiovascular", "Five days after an anterior myocardial infarction, a 66-year-old man suddenly becomes hypotensive with distended neck veins and muffled heart sounds. What is the most likely cause?", ["Papillary muscle rupture", "Ventricular aneurysm", "Fibrinous pericarditis", "Dressler syndrome", "Rupture of the left ventricular free wall"], 4, "From about day 3 to day 14, macrophages remove dead tissue and the wall is weakest, so rupture is most likely. Free wall rupture causes cardiac tamponade (Beck triad). Papillary muscle rupture causes acute mitral regurgitation; Dressler syndrome appears weeks later.", 1, "F", "Pathology"],
["Cardiovascular", "A 78-year-old man has exertional chest pain and fainted while climbing stairs. A harsh crescendo-decrescendo systolic murmur is heard at the right upper sternal border, radiating to the carotids. What is the most likely cause of his murmur?", ["Infective endocarditis of the tricuspid valve", "Age-related calcification of a trileaflet aortic valve", "Myxomatous degeneration of the mitral valve", "Rheumatic fusion of the mitral commissures", "Dilation of the aortic root"], 1, "Aortic stenosis in the elderly is usually from wear-and-tear calcification; in younger patients a bicuspid valve calcifies earlier. Angina, syncope and heart failure are the classic symptoms and signal the need for valve replacement.", 1, "F", "Pathology"],
["Cardiovascular", "A 58-year-old man starts atorvastatin for high LDL cholesterol. A few weeks later he has muscle aches and weakness. What is the mechanism of his drug, and which test should be checked?", ["Binds bile acids in the gut; check vitamin K levels", "Inhibits HMG-CoA reductase; check creatine kinase", "Inhibits PCSK9; check LDL receptors", "Activates PPAR-alpha; check triglycerides", "Inhibits intestinal cholesterol absorption; check liver enzymes"], 1, "Statins block the rate-limiting step of cholesterol synthesis, so liver cells make more LDL receptors and clear more LDL. Myopathy is a key adverse effect; check CK. Risk rises with fibrates and CYP3A4 inhibitors.", 1, "F", "Pharmacology"],
["Cardiovascular", "A 3-year-old boy with tetralogy of Fallot squats during play when he becomes blue and breathless. How does squatting help him?", ["It raises systemic vascular resistance and decreases the right-to-left shunt", "It relieves the pulmonary stenosis", "It lowers pulmonary vascular resistance", "It decreases venous return to the heart", "It closes the ventricular septal defect"], 0, "Squatting kinks the femoral arteries and raises systemic resistance, so more right ventricular blood goes to the lungs instead of across the VSD. Tetralogy comes from anterosuperior displacement of the infundibular septum.", 1, "F", "Physiology"],
["Cardiovascular", "A 3-year-old has had fever for 6 days, red eyes without discharge, cracked lips, a strawberry tongue, a rash, and swollen red hands. Without treatment, which complication is most feared?", ["Coronary artery aneurysm", "Mitral stenosis", "Pulmonary hypertension", "Renal artery stenosis", "Aortic dissection"], 0, "Kawasaki disease is a medium-vessel vasculitis of young children. Treat with IVIG and aspirin to lower the risk of coronary aneurysm, thrombosis and myocardial infarction. It is one of the few times aspirin is given to a child.", 1, "F", "Pathology"],
["Cardiovascular", "A 10-year-old develops migratory polyarthritis, a new murmur and subcutaneous nodules 3 weeks after an untreated sore throat. What is the mechanism of his heart damage?", ["Antibodies against streptococcal M protein cross-react with cardiac proteins", "T-cell response to a viral superantigen", "Immune complexes deposited in the endocardium", "Direct bacterial invasion of the myocardium", "Exotoxin-mediated destruction of myocytes"], 0, "Acute rheumatic fever is a type II hypersensitivity caused by molecular mimicry. The mitral valve is most often affected; chronic disease causes mitral stenosis. Aschoff bodies are characteristic.", 1, "F", "Immunology"],
["Cardiovascular", "Two months after starting lisinopril for hypertension, a 52-year-old woman has a dry, persistent cough. What causes this side effect?", ["Reduced angiotensin II levels in the lung", "Increased histamine release from mast cells", "Direct bronchospasm from beta receptor blockade", "Increased bradykinin levels", "Increased aldosterone levels"], 3, "ACE also breaks down bradykinin. ACE inhibitors raise bradykinin, causing cough and sometimes angioedema. An angiotensin receptor blocker such as losartan is a good substitute because it does not affect bradykinin.", 1, "F", "Pharmacology"],
["Cardiovascular", "A 28-year-old man who injects drugs has fever and a new holosystolic murmur at the left lower sternal border that increases with inspiration. Which organism and valve are most likely?", ["Viridans streptococci infecting the mitral valve", "Staphylococcus epidermidis infecting the aortic valve", "Staphylococcus aureus infecting the tricuspid valve", "Enterococcus infecting the pulmonic valve", "Streptococcus gallolyticus infecting the mitral valve"], 2, "Injection drug use most often causes S. aureus endocarditis of the tricuspid valve; right-sided murmurs get louder with inspiration. Viridans streptococci affect damaged valves after dental work; S. epidermidis affects prosthetic valves.", 1, "F", "Microbiology"],
["Cardiovascular", "A 17-year-old athlete with a family history of sudden death has a systolic murmur at the left sternal border. Which maneuver would make his murmur louder?", ["Valsalva maneuver", "Sustained hand grip", "Lying down from standing", "Passive leg raise", "Squatting"], 0, "In hypertrophic cardiomyopathy the outflow obstruction worsens when the LV is smaller. Valsalva and standing reduce preload, so the murmur gets louder. Squatting, leg raise and hand grip increase preload or afterload and soften it. Most cases are autosomal dominant sarcomere gene mutations.", 1, "C", "Physiology"],
["Respiratory & renal", "A 38-year-old woman who never smoked has worsening shortness of breath. CT shows panacinar emphysema in the lower lobes, and she has signs of liver cirrhosis. What is the most likely cause?", ["Alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency", "Asthma", "Bronchiectasis from Kartagener syndrome", "Chronic bronchitis", "Cystic fibrosis"], 0, "Too little alpha-1 antitrypsin leaves elastase unopposed, destroying alveolar walls throughout the acinus, worst in the lower lobes. Misfolded protein builds up in liver cells (PAS-positive globules) and causes cirrhosis. Smoking causes centriacinar emphysema in the upper lobes.", 1, "F", "Genetics"],
["Respiratory & renal", "A 45-year-old woman develops sudden shortness of breath and chest pain 3 days after hip surgery. Which arterial blood gas pattern is most likely?", ["Low PaO2, low PaCO2, high pH and increased A-a gradient", "Low PaO2, normal PaCO2, normal pH and normal A-a gradient", "Low PaO2, high PaCO2, low pH and normal A-a gradient", "Normal PaO2, low PaCO2, high pH and normal A-a gradient", "High PaO2, high PaCO2, low pH and increased A-a gradient"], 0, "Pulmonary embolism causes V/Q mismatch, so oxygen falls and the A-a gradient rises. The patient breathes fast and blows off CO2, giving a respiratory alkalosis. A normal A-a gradient with high CO2 suggests hypoventilation.", 1, "F", "Physiology"],
["Respiratory & renal", "A 35-year-old Black woman has a dry cough, bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy and a serum calcium of 11.8 mg/dL. Biopsy shows noncaseating granulomas. What causes her hypercalcemia?", ["Reduced renal calcium excretion caused by thiazides", "Osteolytic bone lesions", "Increased 1-alpha-hydroxylase activity in granuloma macrophages", "Parathyroid hormone-related peptide from the tumor", "Increased parathyroid hormone secretion"], 2, "In sarcoidosis, activated macrophages convert 25-hydroxyvitamin D to active 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D, increasing gut calcium absorption. Serum ACE is often elevated. PTHrP causes hypercalcemia in squamous cell carcinoma.", 1, "F", "Pathology"],
["Respiratory & renal", "A man starts isoniazid, rifampin, pyrazinamide and ethambutol for pulmonary tuberculosis. Which vitamin should be added to prevent peripheral neuropathy?", ["Folic acid", "Vitamin D", "Thiamine (vitamin B1)", "Cobalamin (vitamin B12)", "Pyridoxine (vitamin B6)"], 4, "Isoniazid inhibits mycolic acid synthesis and depletes vitamin B6, causing neuropathy. Other effects include hepatotoxicity and drug-induced lupus. Rifampin turns body fluids orange; ethambutol can cause optic neuritis.", 1, "F", "Pharmacology"],
["Respiratory & renal", "A 62-year-old man with a 50 pack-year smoking history has a central lung mass and a serum sodium of 124 mEq/L with concentrated urine. Which tumor type is most likely?", ["Squamous cell carcinoma", "Adenocarcinoma", "Large cell carcinoma", "Small cell carcinoma", "Bronchial carcinoid"], 3, "Small cell lung cancer is central, strongly tied to smoking and arises from neuroendocrine cells. It can secrete ADH (SIADH) or ACTH, and cause Lambert-Eaton syndrome. Squamous cell carcinoma is also central but causes hypercalcemia through PTHrP.", 1, "F", "Pathology"],
["Respiratory & renal", "A baby born at 30 weeks' gestation to a mother with poorly controlled diabetes develops grunting, retractions and cyanosis shortly after birth. Deficiency of a substance made by which cells is responsible?", ["Type I pneumocytes", "Alveolar macrophages", "Club cells", "Goblet cells", "Type II pneumocytes"], 4, "Neonatal respiratory distress syndrome is caused by too little surfactant (mainly dipalmitoylphosphatidylcholine), made by type II pneumocytes from about week 26. High fetal insulin in maternal diabetes delays surfactant production. Type I pneumocytes form the gas exchange surface.", 1, "F", "Histology & cell biology"],
["Respiratory & renal", "A 4-year-old boy has periorbital and leg swelling. Urine shows 4+ protein and no blood. Light microscopy of a kidney biopsy is normal. Which finding is expected on electron microscopy?", ["Effacement of podocyte foot processes", "Mesangial IgA deposits", "Splitting and thinning of the glomerular basement membrane", "Subepithelial immune complex humps", "Spike and dome appearance of the basement membrane"], 0, "Minimal change disease is the most common nephrotic syndrome in children and responds well to corticosteroids. Only EM shows the damage: flattened podocyte foot processes. Subepithelial humps are post-streptococcal GN; spike and dome is membranous nephropathy.", 1, "F", "Pathology"],
["Respiratory & renal", "Two weeks after a sore throat, an 8-year-old has cola-colored urine, puffy eyes and high blood pressure. Serum C3 is low. Which finding is expected on electron microscopy?", ["Subepithelial immune complex humps", "Effacement of podocyte foot processes only", "Splitting of the basement membrane", "Linear IgG along the basement membrane", "Mesangial IgA deposits"], 0, "Post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis is a type III hypersensitivity. Immune complexes deposit beneath the epithelium and activate complement, lowering C3. Linear IgG is anti-GBM disease; mesangial IgA is IgA nephropathy, which appears within days of an infection.", 1, "F", "Immunology"],
["Respiratory & renal", "A patient with heart failure gets IV furosemide. Where does this drug act?", ["Carbonic anhydrase in the proximal tubule", "Na-Cl cotransporter in the distal convoluted tubule", "Epithelial sodium channel in the collecting duct", "Aldosterone receptor in the collecting duct", "Na-K-2Cl cotransporter in the thick ascending limb"], 4, "Loop diuretics block NKCC2, removing the gradient that concentrates urine; they are the most powerful diuretics. Side effects include hypokalemia, ototoxicity and loss of calcium. Thiazides act on the DCT; amiloride blocks ENaC; spironolactone blocks the aldosterone receptor.", 1, "F", "Pharmacology"],
["Respiratory & renal", "A man with 3 days of watery diarrhea has these labs: pH 7.30, PaCO2 30 mm Hg, HCO3- 15 mEq/L, Na+ 140 mEq/L, Cl- 115 mEq/L. Which disorder is present?", ["Primary respiratory acidosis", "High anion gap metabolic acidosis", "Normal anion gap metabolic acidosis", "Metabolic alkalosis", "Primary respiratory alkalosis"], 2, "Low pH with low HCO3- is metabolic acidosis, with a lower PaCO2 as compensation. Anion gap = 140 - (115 + 15) = 10, which is normal. Diarrhea loses bicarbonate and the kidney keeps chloride instead. DKA, lactic acidosis and methanol raise the anion gap.", 1, "F", "Physiology"],
["Respiratory & renal", "Which substance's renal clearance is used to estimate effective renal plasma flow?", ["Urea", "Glucose", "Creatinine", "Para-aminohippuric acid (PAH)", "Inulin"], 3, "PAH is filtered and almost completely secreted by the proximal tubule, so nearly all of it is removed in one pass through the kidney. Its clearance approximates renal plasma flow. Inulin is filtered but neither reabsorbed nor secreted, so its clearance equals GFR.", 1, "F", "Physiology"],
["Respiratory & renal", "A 40-year-old man has flank pain, hematuria, hypertension and enlarged kidneys filled with cysts. His mother died of a subarachnoid hemorrhage. Mutation of which gene is most likely?", ["PKHD1 on chromosome 6", "PKD1 on chromosome 16", "VHL on chromosome 3", "TSC2 on chromosome 16", "WT1 on chromosome 11"], 1, "Autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease is most often due to PKD1 (polycystin-1). It is associated with berry aneurysms, liver cysts and mitral valve prolapse. PKHD1 causes the autosomal recessive childhood form.", 1, "F", "Genetics"],
["Respiratory & renal", "A 60-year-old smoker has hematuria, flank pain and a renal mass. His hematocrit is 56%. Which tumor is most likely, and why is his hematocrit high?", ["Adrenal adenoma secreting cortisol", "Transitional cell carcinoma causing hemoconcentration", "Renal angiomyolipoma secreting renin", "Wilms tumor secreting erythropoietin", "Renal cell carcinoma secreting erythropoietin"], 4, "Renal cell carcinoma comes from proximal tubule cells; smoking is a key risk factor and VHL loss on chromosome 3p is common. It can make EPO, PTHrP, ACTH or renin (paraneoplastic syndromes) and tends to invade the renal vein.", 1, "F", "Pathology"],
["Gastrointestinal", "A 25-year-old woman has bloating, diarrhea, iron deficiency and itchy blisters on her elbows. Duodenal biopsy shows villous atrophy and crypt hyperplasia. Which test is most useful to support the diagnosis?", ["IgA anti-tissue transglutaminase antibody", "Anti-mitochondrial antibody", "Anti-Saccharomyces cerevisiae antibody", "Perinuclear ANCA", "Fecal calprotectin"], 0, "Celiac disease is an immune reaction to gluten (gliadin) in people with HLA-DQ2 or DQ8. Anti-tTG IgA is the best screening test; check total IgA because IgA deficiency is common in celiac disease. The rash is dermatitis herpetiformis.", 1, "F", "Immunology"],
["Gastrointestinal", "A 24-year-old man has abdominal pain, diarrhea and a perianal fistula. Colonoscopy shows patchy ulcers with normal mucosa between them, and biopsy shows transmural inflammation with noncaseating granulomas. What is the diagnosis?", ["Ulcerative colitis", "Crohn disease", "Celiac disease", "Intestinal tuberculosis", "Pseudomembranous colitis"], 1, "Crohn disease can affect any part of the GI tract, most often the terminal ileum. It has skip lesions, transmural inflammation (fistulas, strictures) and noncaseating granulomas. Ulcerative colitis is continuous from the rectum and limited to the mucosa.", 1, "F", "Pathology"],
["Gastrointestinal", "A health care worker has these hepatitis B results: HBsAg negative, anti-HBs positive, anti-HBc negative. What do these results show?", ["Window period of acute infection", "Acute infection", "Chronic infection", "Immunity from vaccination", "Immunity from past infection"], 3, "The vaccine contains only HBsAg, so it produces anti-HBs alone. Past infection gives both anti-HBs and anti-HBc. The window period has anti-HBc IgM with neither HBsAg nor anti-HBs.", 1, "F", "Microbiology"],
["Gastrointestinal", "A 45-year-old man has a duodenal ulcer. A breath test after he swallows labeled urea is positive. Which bacterial enzyme lets this organism survive in the stomach?", ["Beta-lactamase", "Catalase", "Hyaluronidase", "Urease", "Coagulase"], 3, "Helicobacter pylori breaks urea into ammonia, which neutralizes acid around the bacterium; the urea breath test detects this. H. pylori causes most duodenal ulcers and raises the risk of gastric adenocarcinoma and MALT lymphoma.", 1, "F", "Microbiology"],
["Gastrointestinal", "A man with reflux esophagitis starts omeprazole. What is its mechanism of action?", ["Formation of a protective coat over ulcers", "Activation of prostaglandin E1 receptors", "Irreversible inhibition of the H+/K+ ATPase in parietal cells", "Reversible block of H2 histamine receptors on parietal cells", "Neutralization of stomach acid"], 2, "Proton pump inhibitors block the last step of acid secretion. Long-term use is linked to C. difficile infection, pneumonia, low magnesium and reduced B12 and iron absorption. H2 blockers (famotidine) are reversible; misoprostol is a PGE1 analog.", 1, "F", "Pharmacology"],
["Gastrointestinal", "A healthy 22-year-old student notices her eyes turn yellow during exam week when she skips meals. Her unconjugated bilirubin is mildly elevated and other liver tests and blood counts are normal. What is the cause?", ["Hemolysis from a red cell enzyme defect", "Absent UDP-glucuronosyltransferase activity", "Mildly decreased UDP-glucuronosyltransferase activity", "Obstruction of the common bile duct", "Defective secretion of conjugated bilirubin into bile"], 2, "Gilbert syndrome is common and harmless; fasting, stress or illness bring out mild unconjugated jaundice. Absent enzyme is Crigler-Najjar type I. Defective excretion of conjugated bilirubin is Dubin-Johnson syndrome.", 1, "F", "Biochemistry"],
["Gastrointestinal", "A newborn has not passed meconium by 48 hours and has a distended abdomen. Rectal biopsy shows no ganglion cells. What is the embryologic cause?", ["Incomplete canalization of the duodenum", "Failure of the urorectal septum to develop", "Failure of the midgut to rotate", "Failed migration of neural crest cells into the distal colon", "Persistence of the vitelline duct"], 3, "Hirschsprung disease lacks both the Meissner and Auerbach plexuses in the rectum and nearby colon, so that segment cannot relax. It is linked to RET mutations and Down syndrome. A persistent vitelline duct causes Meckel diverticulum.", 1, "F", "Embryology"],
["Gastrointestinal", "A 19-year-old woman took a large overdose of acetaminophen 6 hours ago. Which antidote should be given, and how does it work?", ["Fomepizole, which inhibits alcohol dehydrogenase", "Flumazenil, which blocks benzodiazepine receptors", "Deferoxamine, which binds iron", "Naloxone, which blocks opioid receptors", "N-acetylcysteine, which restores glutathione"], 4, "In overdose, more acetaminophen is turned by CYP2E1 into the toxic metabolite NAPQI. Glutathione runs out and liver cells die (centrilobular necrosis). N-acetylcysteine restores glutathione and works best within 8 to 10 hours.", 1, "F", "Pharmacology"],
["Reproductive & endocrine", "A 30-year-old woman has weight loss, heat intolerance, a diffusely enlarged thyroid, and protruding eyes. TSH is low and free T4 is high. What causes her disease?", ["Autonomous nodules from a TSH receptor mutation", "Antibodies against thyroid peroxidase that destroy the gland", "Viral inflammation of the thyroid", "A TSH-secreting pituitary adenoma", "Antibodies that stimulate the TSH receptor"], 4, "Graves disease is a type II hypersensitivity in which antibodies act like TSH. The same antibodies stimulate fibroblasts behind the eye and in the skin, causing exophthalmos and pretibial myxedema.", 1, "F", "Immunology"],
["Reproductive & endocrine", "A 50-year-old woman has fatigue, weight gain and cold intolerance with a painless, firm thyroid. TSH is high and anti-thyroid peroxidase antibodies are present. What does a thyroid biopsy show?", ["Dense fibrosis extending into neck muscles", "Papillae with Orphan Annie eye nuclei", "Tall follicular cells with scalloped colloid", "Granulomas with multinucleated giant cells", "Lymphocytes forming germinal centers and Hurthle cells"], 4, "Hashimoto thyroiditis is the most common cause of hypothyroidism in iodine-sufficient areas and raises the risk of thyroid lymphoma. Granulomas are de Quervain thyroiditis; dense fibrosis is Riedel thyroiditis; Orphan Annie nuclei are papillary carcinoma.", 1, "F", "Pathology"],
["Reproductive & endocrine", "A 14-year-old with type 1 diabetes stopped taking insulin and now has vomiting, deep rapid breathing and a fruity breath odor. Increased activity of which enzyme in adipose tissue supplies substrate for her ketones?", ["Glycogen synthase", "Hormone-sensitive lipase", "Lipoprotein lipase", "HMG-CoA reductase", "Acetyl-CoA carboxylase"], 1, "Without insulin, hormone-sensitive lipase is no longer suppressed. It releases fatty acids that the liver oxidizes into ketone bodies (acetoacetate and beta-hydroxybutyrate). Lipoprotein lipase, used to store fat, needs insulin.", 1, "C", "Biochemistry"],
["Reproductive & endocrine", "A 54-year-old obese man with newly diagnosed type 2 diabetes starts the first-line oral drug. It should be avoided if his kidney function falls sharply. What is its main mechanism?", ["Decreases glucose production by the liver", "Increases insulin release from beta cells", "Slows carbohydrate absorption in the gut", "Activates PPAR-gamma in fat and muscle", "Blocks glucose reabsorption in the proximal tubule"], 0, "Metformin is a biguanide that reduces hepatic gluconeogenesis and improves insulin sensitivity without causing hypoglycemia or weight gain. Its rare serious risk is lactic acidosis, more likely with kidney failure. Sulfonylureas increase insulin release; SGLT2 inhibitors block renal glucose reabsorption.", 1, "F", "Pharmacology"],
["Reproductive & endocrine", "A woman has central obesity, purple striae and high blood pressure. Her ACTH is high, and cortisol falls after a high-dose dexamethasone test. What is the most likely cause?", ["Ectopic ACTH from a small cell lung cancer", "An adrenal carcinoma", "Long-term prednisone treatment", "An ACTH-secreting pituitary adenoma", "An adrenal adenoma"], 3, "High ACTH means the cause is ACTH-dependent. A pituitary adenoma (Cushing disease) still responds partly to feedback, so high-dose dexamethasone suppresses it; ectopic ACTH does not. Adrenal tumors and steroid medicines suppress ACTH.", 1, "C", "Physiology"],
["Reproductive & endocrine", "A newborn girl has ambiguous genitalia. On day 10 she vomits, and labs show low sodium and high potassium. Which hormone precursor is most elevated?", ["Corticosterone", "Aldosterone", "11-deoxycorticosterone", "17-hydroxyprogesterone", "Cortisol"], 3, "21-hydroxylase deficiency is the most common congenital adrenal hyperplasia. Without it the adrenal cannot make aldosterone or cortisol (salt wasting), and the precursors (such as 17-hydroxyprogesterone) are shunted to androgens, virilizing girls. 11-beta-hydroxylase deficiency raises 11-deoxycorticosterone and causes hypertension.", 1, "F", "Biochemistry"],
["Reproductive & endocrine", "A 42-year-old man has blood pressure of 170/100 mm Hg despite 3 drugs, and a potassium of 2.9 mEq/L. Plasma aldosterone is high and renin is low. What is the most likely diagnosis?", ["Renal artery stenosis", "Primary hyperaldosteronism", "Liddle syndrome", "Cushing syndrome", "Pheochromocytoma"], 1, "An adrenal adenoma or bilateral adrenal hyperplasia makes aldosterone on its own, causing hypertension and hypokalemia; the resulting volume expansion suppresses renin. Renal artery stenosis raises both renin and aldosterone. Liddle syndrome has low renin AND low aldosterone.", 1, "F", "Physiology"],
["Reproductive & endocrine", "A 35-year-old woman has episodes of headache, sweating and palpitations with very high blood pressure. Urine metanephrines are high and CT shows an adrenal mass. Before surgery, which drug should be started first?", ["Phenoxybenzamine", "Spironolactone", "Lisinopril", "Propranolol", "Hydrochlorothiazide"], 0, "In pheochromocytoma, give an irreversible alpha blocker first. Starting a beta blocker alone leaves alpha receptors unopposed, and blood pressure can rise dangerously. A beta blocker can be added later for rapid heart rate.", 1, "F", "Pharmacology"],
["Reproductive & endocrine", "A 26-year-old woman has irregular periods, acne, excess facial hair and obesity. Ultrasound shows enlarged ovaries with many small follicles. Without treatment, which cancer is she most at risk of?", ["Cervical squamous cell carcinoma", "Ovarian serous carcinoma", "Endometrial carcinoma", "Choriocarcinoma", "Breast carcinoma in her 20s"], 2, "In polycystic ovary syndrome, anovulation means no progesterone. The endometrium is exposed to estrogen without progesterone, raising the risk of hyperplasia and carcinoma. PCOS also involves insulin resistance and a high LH-to-FSH ratio.", 1, "F", "Pathology"],
["Reproductive & endocrine", "A tall 20-year-old man has small firm testes, gynecomastia and infertility. FSH and LH are high and testosterone is low. What is the most likely karyotype?", ["46,XY with androgen receptor mutation", "46,XX with SRY translocation", "47,XXY", "47,XYY", "45,X"], 2, "Klinefelter syndrome: seminiferous tubules are damaged, so inhibin falls and FSH rises; Leydig cell function falls, so testosterone falls and LH rises. Androgen insensitivity (46,XY) gives a female appearance with normal or high testosterone.", 1, "F", "Genetics"],
["Reproductive & endocrine", "During the first weeks of pregnancy, which hormone keeps the corpus luteum producing progesterone, and which cells make it?", ["Human chorionic gonadotropin from syncytiotrophoblast", "Human chorionic gonadotropin from cytotrophoblast", "Progesterone from the placenta", "Human placental lactogen from decidual cells", "Luteinizing hormone from the anterior pituitary"], 0, "hCG acts like LH and supports the corpus luteum until the placenta can make enough progesterone (about weeks 8 to 10). It is the hormone measured in pregnancy tests. Very high hCG suggests a molar pregnancy or choriocarcinoma.", 1, "F", "Embryology"],
["Reproductive & endocrine", "A 48-year-old woman with HER2-positive breast cancer starts trastuzumab. Which test should be monitored during treatment because of a known adverse effect?", ["Bone density scan", "Echocardiogram to check left ventricular ejection fraction", "Serum calcium", "Pulmonary function tests", "Audiometry"], 1, "Trastuzumab is a monoclonal antibody against HER2. It can reduce heart function, usually reversibly, so ejection fraction is checked. Doxorubicin also causes cardiotoxicity but it is dose dependent and usually irreversible.", 1, "F", "Pharmacology"],
["Reproductive & endocrine", "A 15-year-old girl is short, has a webbed neck, widely spaced nipples and has not started puberty. Karyotype is 45,X. Which heart defect is most common in her condition?", ["Transposition of the great arteries", "Ebstein anomaly", "Bicuspid aortic valve", "Tetralogy of Fallot", "Endocardial cushion defect"], 2, "Turner syndrome causes streak ovaries (primary amenorrhea), lymphedema and a horseshoe kidney. The most common cardiac defect is a bicuspid aortic valve; preductal coarctation of the aorta is also common. Endocardial cushion defects are linked to Down syndrome.", 1, "F", "Genetics"],
["Reproductive & endocrine", "A 30-year-old woman has no periods for 6 months and milky discharge from both breasts. Pregnancy test is negative, prolactin is very high, and MRI shows a small pituitary adenoma. Which drug is first-line treatment?", ["Cabergoline", "Levothyroxine", "Leuprolide", "Haloperidol", "Octreotide"], 0, "Prolactinomas usually shrink with dopamine agonists (cabergoline or bromocriptine), because dopamine inhibits prolactin release. Haloperidol blocks dopamine and would raise prolactin. Octreotide treats acromegaly.", 1, "F", "Pharmacology"],
["Multisystem", "An older man who lives alone and eats only tea and toast has bleeding gums, poor wound healing and small hemorrhages around hair follicles with corkscrew hairs. Which reaction is impaired?", ["Cleavage of procollagen peptides", "Glycosylation of collagen in the Golgi", "Gamma-carboxylation of clotting factors", "Hydroxylation of proline and lysine residues in collagen", "Cross-linking of collagen by lysyl oxidase"], 3, "Scurvy is vitamin C deficiency. Vitamin C is needed by prolyl and lysyl hydroxylases; without it collagen triple helices are unstable and vessels are fragile. Copper is needed for lysyl oxidase; vitamin K for gamma-carboxylation.", 1, "F", "Nutrition"],
["Multisystem", "A 2-year-old in a famine area has a swollen belly, leg edema, skin lesions and an enlarged fatty liver, though she is not severely wasted. What is the most likely diagnosis?", ["Kwashiorkor", "Rickets", "Marasmus", "Beriberi", "Pellagra"], 0, "Kwashiorkor is protein deficiency with some calorie intake. Low albumin causes edema, and too little apolipoprotein makes fat build up in the liver. Marasmus is total calorie deficiency with severe wasting and no edema.", 1, "F", "Nutrition"],
["Multisystem", "A patient with E. coli urosepsis develops fever, hypotension and organ failure. Which bacterial component triggers this response, and through which receptor?", ["Teichoic acid through MHC class II", "Capsule polysaccharide through complement receptor 1", "Lipid A of lipopolysaccharide through toll-like receptor 4", "Flagellin through CD14 alone", "Peptidoglycan through toll-like receptor 9"], 2, "Endotoxin (LPS) is in the outer membrane of gram-negative bacteria; lipid A is the toxic part. It binds CD14 and TLR4 on macrophages, which release TNF-alpha, IL-1 and IL-6, causing fever and shock. TLR9 recognizes unmethylated CpG DNA.", 1, "C", "Microbiology"],
["Multisystem", "A 58-year-old woman has painless swelling of many lymph nodes. Biopsy shows small cleaved B cells in nodular follicles. Cytogenetics show t(14;18). What does this translocation do?", ["Inactivates the p53 tumor suppressor", "Overexpresses BCL-2, which blocks apoptosis", "Overexpresses c-MYC, which drives the cell cycle", "Overexpresses cyclin D1, which drives the cell cycle", "Creates the BCR-ABL tyrosine kinase"], 1, "Follicular lymphoma moves BCL-2 next to the immunoglobulin heavy chain promoter. BCL-2 keeps the mitochondrial membrane intact, preventing cytochrome c release and the intrinsic pathway of apoptosis. c-MYC t(8;14) is Burkitt; cyclin D1 t(11;14) is mantle cell lymphoma.", 1, "F", "Pathology"],
["Multisystem", "In a family with a rare disease, an affected father passes the disease to all of his daughters and none of his sons. What is the most likely inheritance pattern?", ["Autosomal dominant", "Autosomal recessive", "X-linked recessive", "X-linked dominant", "Mitochondrial"], 3, "A father gives his X chromosome to every daughter and his Y to every son. If one X copy is enough to cause disease, all daughters are affected and no sons. Mitochondrial diseases pass only from the mother. An example is hypophosphatemic rickets.", 1, "F", "Genetics"],
["Multisystem", "An autosomal recessive disease affects 1 in 10,000 people in a population in Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium. About how common are carriers?", ["1 in 10,000", "1 in 100", "1 in 5,000", "1 in 50", "1 in 200"], 3, "q squared = 1/10,000, so q = 1/100 and p is about 1. Carrier frequency = 2pq, about 2 x 1/100 = 1/50.", 1, "C", "Genetics"],
["Multisystem", "A 25-year-old Ashkenazi Jewish man has an enlarged spleen and liver, bone pain and low platelets. Bone marrow shows macrophages that look like crumpled tissue paper. Which enzyme is deficient?", ["Sphingomyelinase", "Glucocerebrosidase", "Hexosaminidase A", "Alpha-galactosidase A", "Galactocerebrosidase"], 1, "Gaucher disease is the most common lysosomal storage disease and is autosomal recessive. Glucocerebroside builds up in macrophages. Hexosaminidase A is Tay-Sachs; sphingomyelinase is Niemann-Pick; alpha-galactosidase A is Fabry disease.", 1, "F", "Biochemistry"],
["Multisystem", "A 30-year-old woman has had breast cancer, her brother had a sarcoma at 20, and her mother had a brain tumor at 40. A germline mutation in which gene is most likely?", ["TP53", "MLH1", "BRCA1", "RB1", "APC"], 0, "Li-Fraumeni syndrome is an autosomal dominant loss of p53, the guardian of the genome. It causes many cancers at young ages: sarcomas, breast cancer, leukemia, adrenal and brain tumors. BRCA1 mainly causes breast and ovarian cancer.", 1, "F", "Genetics"],
["Multisystem", "A 66-year-old man with multiple myeloma has heavy proteinuria, an enlarged tongue and a stiff, thick heart. A fat pad biopsy shows apple-green birefringence with Congo red under polarized light. What is the deposited protein?", ["Serum amyloid A", "Beta-2 microglobulin", "Immunoglobulin light chains", "Amyloid precursor protein", "Transthyretin"], 2, "AL amyloidosis comes from plasma cell light chains and is linked to myeloma. Serum amyloid A deposits in chronic inflammation (AA amyloidosis); beta-2 microglobulin deposits in long-term dialysis; transthyretin causes senile cardiac amyloidosis.", 1, "F", "Pathology"],
["Multisystem", "Which drug is eliminated at a constant amount per hour (zero-order kinetics) regardless of its concentration?", ["Digoxin", "Ethanol", "Penicillin", "Lithium", "Gentamicin"], 1, "In zero-order elimination the enzymes are saturated, so the same amount is removed per unit time and there is no fixed half-life. Ethanol, phenytoin and high-dose aspirin are the classic examples. Most drugs follow first-order kinetics.", 1, "F", "Pharmacology"],
["Biostatistics & epidemiology", "A new screening test is used on 100 people with a disease and 200 people without it. It is positive in 90 people with the disease and in 20 people without it. What is the positive predictive value?", ["About 10%", "About 95%", "About 18%", "About 82%", "90%"], 3, "PPV = true positives / all positives = 90 / (90 + 20) = 90 / 110, about 82%. Sensitivity = 90/100 = 90%, and specificity = 180/200 = 90%. Unlike sensitivity and specificity, PPV changes with disease prevalence.", 1, "F", "Behavioral science"],
["Biostatistics & epidemiology", "In a trial, 20% of patients in the placebo group and 15% in the treatment group have a stroke over 5 years. How many patients must be treated to prevent one stroke?", ["5", "20", "100", "33", "15"], 1, "Absolute risk reduction = 20% - 15% = 5% = 0.05. Number needed to treat = 1 / ARR = 1 / 0.05 = 20. The relative risk reduction (25%) sounds larger but does not give NNT.", 1, "F", "Behavioral science"],
["Biostatistics & epidemiology", "Researchers find 200 patients with pancreatic cancer and 400 people without it, then ask both groups about past coffee drinking. Which measure of association is most appropriate?", ["Hazard ratio", "Relative risk", "Incidence rate", "Attributable risk", "Odds ratio"], 4, "This is a case-control study: groups are chosen by outcome and exposure is looked at backward. Incidence cannot be calculated, so the odds ratio is used. Cohort studies follow exposure groups forward and use relative risk.", 1, "F", "Behavioral science"],
["Biostatistics & epidemiology", "A study concludes that a drug lowers mortality, but in truth the drug has no effect. Which error has occurred?", ["Confounding by indication", "Type II error", "Recall bias", "Type I error", "Lead-time bias"], 3, "A type I error (false positive) rejects a null hypothesis that is true; its probability is alpha, usually 0.05. A type II error (false negative) misses a real difference; its probability is beta, and power = 1 - beta.", 1, "F", "Behavioral science"],
["Biostatistics & epidemiology", "A new screening program finds lung cancer earlier, and patients now live 2 years longer after diagnosis. However, the age at death is unchanged. Which bias explains the apparent benefit?", ["Recall bias", "Length-time bias", "Lead-time bias", "Observer bias", "Selection bias"], 2, "Lead-time bias: earlier diagnosis adds time to survival measured from diagnosis without delaying death. Use mortality rates to avoid it. Length-time bias happens because screening finds slow-growing tumors more easily.", 1, "F", "Behavioral science"],
["Communication & ethics", "A 16-year-old girl asks her doctor for oral contraceptives and asks that her parents not be told. What is the most appropriate response?", ["Refuse until she is 18", "Prescribe only if she agrees to tell her parents", "Tell her parental consent is required", "Call her parents to discuss the request", "Counsel her and prescribe contraception while keeping the visit confidential"], 4, "In most US states, minors can consent to contraception, STI care and prenatal care without their parents. Encourage her to involve a trusted adult, but do not require it or break confidentiality.", 1, "F", "Behavioral science"],
["Communication & ethics", "A 40-year-old Jehovah's Witness with full decision-making capacity is bleeding after surgery and refuses a blood transfusion, knowing he may die. What should the physician do?", ["Ask his family to make the decision", "Obtain a court order to transfuse", "Respect his refusal and use other treatments that he accepts", "Transfuse anyway because his life is in danger", "Transfuse after he loses consciousness"], 2, "An adult with capacity may refuse any treatment, even life-saving treatment. Offer acceptable alternatives (volume expanders, cell saver if accepted). Courts may order transfusion for a child, not for a competent adult.", 1, "F", "Behavioral science"],
["Communication & ethics", "A woman who speaks only Dari comes for a new diagnosis discussion. Her 12-year-old son speaks fluent English and offers to translate. What is the best approach?", ["Give her written information in English", "Use a trained medical interpreter", "Let her son translate", "Speak slowly and use simple English", "Use a staff member who speaks some Dari"], 1, "Professional medical interpreters are trained to be accurate and keep confidentiality. Family members, especially children, may leave things out or change the meaning, and the role is unfair to a child.", 1, "F", "Behavioral science"],
["Communication & ethics", "A physician must tell a patient that her biopsy shows cancer. After making sure they have privacy and time, what should the physician do first?", ["Give her the pathology report to read", "State the diagnosis right away in one sentence", "Describe all the treatment options", "Tell her family first", "Ask the patient what she understands about why the biopsy was done"], 4, "The SPIKES method: Setting, Perception (what the patient knows), Invitation (how much she wants to know), Knowledge (share the news in plain words), Emotions (respond with empathy), Strategy and summary.", 1, "F", "Behavioral science"],
["Communication & ethics", "A resident realizes she gave a patient a double dose of a medication. The patient had no harm. What should she do?", ["Tell only the attending physician", "Wait to see if a complication develops", "Not tell the patient because there was no harm", "Tell the patient what happened and apologize", "Change the medical record to show the correct dose"], 3, "Physicians should disclose errors to patients honestly, whether or not harm occurred, and report them through the hospital's safety system. Changing records is never acceptable.", 1, "F", "Behavioral science"],
["Communication & ethics", "A man is newly diagnosed with HIV. He refuses to tell his wife because he is afraid she will leave him. What is the best next step?", ["Respect his wish and tell no one", "Stop treating him until he tells her", "Encourage him to tell her and explain that the health department may notify her if he does not", "Tell his employer", "Call his wife right away without telling him"], 2, "Confidentiality can be broken to protect an identifiable third party at serious risk. First counsel and support the patient to disclose. If he still refuses, report to public health, which can notify the partner without naming him.", 1, "F", "Behavioral science"]
);
